1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,208
When a user loses the phone that holds
their authenticator app,

2
00:00:05,208 --> 00:00:11,411
reset its OTP credential in three moves,
in this order: sign the user out,

3
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:15,372
require a new authenticator, then delete
the old one.

4
00:00:15,372 --> 00:00:19,200
This is Keycloak 26.8.2.

5
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:26,210
Before you start, confirm that the request
comes from the user,

6
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:31,006
through a contact you had before the phone
was lost.

7
00:00:31,006 --> 00:00:38,620
Then sign in to the admin console with an
account that can manage the realm’s users.

8
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:44,180
Check that the user’s realm is the current
realm.

9
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:49,483
Select Users, search for the user, and
select their username.

10
00:00:57,733 --> 00:01:02,892
On the Sessions tab, select Logout all
sessions, and then Confirm.

11
00:01:02,892 --> 00:01:09,283
An open session would otherwise reach the
authenticator setup without the password.

12
00:01:10,467 --> 00:01:16,461
On the Details tab, in Required user
actions, choose Configure OTP.

13
00:01:16,461 --> 00:01:17,862
Then select Save.

14
00:01:17,862 --> 00:01:20,587
Do this before you delete anything.

15
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:26,889
Select the Credentials tab.

16
00:01:26,889 --> 00:01:33,703
The OTP row is the lost phone: its label
names the device, Alex’s phone.

17
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:38,830
Open the row’s menu, and select Delete.

18
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:43,958
Confirm with Delete.

19
00:01:43,958 --> 00:01:46,310
The phone’s row is gone.

20
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:51,197
At the next sign-in, after the password,

21
00:01:51,197 --> 00:01:55,819
Keycloak asks the user to set up an
authenticator.

22
00:01:55,819 --> 00:02:03,479
They scan the QR code with the app on
their new phone, type the code it shows,

23
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:06,670
name the device, and select Submit.

24
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:17,320
To check, open the Credentials tab again.

25
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:23,212
The new OTP row has the new device’s name
and today’s date.

26
00:02:23,212 --> 00:02:26,713
And the admin events record the deletion.

27
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:40,664
The written guide has every step, and what
to do when a code is rejected.

28
00:02:40,664 --> 00:02:45,397
Find it in the library at
karandeshmukh.dev.
